WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
Super Mario Brothers 2!

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.900
Mario's back, bigger and badder than ever!

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:28.180
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.100
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.000
I'm Sean. I'm Joe.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.980
And I'm Sam. Welcome back, Sam!

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.060
Hey, it's good to be back.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.980
I thought you died in the war.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.120
I respawned.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.100
As everybody does.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.820
I think we have something that we have to get out of the way here, though, and it has nothing to do with Sam, unfortunately.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.060
It just has to do with this whole Super Mario Brothers 2 conversation.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:58.260
And that is that there is a Super Mario Brothers 2, but it's not quite the one we're playing today.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:08.120
It was released in Japan, and it's just a much harder version of Super Mario Brothers 1 with, I think, like a poison mushroom power-up as well and stuff like that.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.760
And, you know, we may eventually play that for the show, but not today.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.260
Today, we're playing Doki Doki Panic.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.200
Okay, but not really either.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:31.040
We're playing a, you know, like a remixed version of Doki Doki Panic, which came out on the Famicom, and it came out as Super Mario Brothers 2, referred to as Super Mario Brothers USA in Japan.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.160
And that is the game we will be playing today.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.740
Everybody got that? Okay, okay.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:41.500
So Doki Doki Literature Club was also an expansion pack for Super Mario Brothers 1.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.500
I got it.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:50.320
Yeah, the references, if you pick up on them, they're subtle, but you can definitely see how Doki Doki Literature Club and Super Mario Brothers share like an extended universe.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:59.940
So, you know, guys, I think we have to talk a little bit about the first game in this series, Super Mario Brothers, since that was a huge episode for us.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.160
It was an early episode.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.180
It was one of the original launch games on the system.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.360
And, you know, we pretty much reference every single platformer.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.540
We talk about the game again.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:14.200
So can you guys just briefly go around and remind me why you liked the game so much?

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.480
You want to start with you, Joe? Sure.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:30.440
Well, first of all, I think for all of the other games that we talk about where we reference Super Mario Brothers, I don't think that anything has really reached that level of platformer that Super Mario Brothers, you know, kind of introduced us to.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.380
Like, I don't think anything has felt as clean or as polished yet as Super Mario Brothers.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.460
And if that's not entirely true, if there is an exception, it's in a totally different way that it's polished.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:55.240
There's just something about the feel of Super Mario Brothers, the feel of the jump and the feel of the level design that kind of makes it harder for other platforms to be judged properly or to be judged in a good light because it's so well done.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.260
What about you, Sam?

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:03.860
Yeah, I'd also add that it's the way that enemies spawn and the way that levels are laid out hasn't really been matched.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:10.340
And in that capacity of the first one where each enemy is placed so that you can jump on them a very specific way.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.000
And it seems like the whole game is just expertly planned.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.180
Yeah. And Sean?

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.220
I mean, I'm just going to echo what Dave said for the most part.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.120
It has a level of artisanship that it's really hard to fully describe.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.560
That, again, just can't, it hasn't been replicated since.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:37.540
There's been games that I've been more excited about because they still have that air of novelty.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.300
But Super Mario Brothers was just so much of a classic and it hasn't really been lived up to since.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:04:01.420
Yeah, I think one thing that we said a lot in between these like 100 plus episodes between the two games is that a lot of these games feel like they're inspired by Super Mario Brothers, but they add an extra element or try to add in an additional genre working its way into the platformer.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.560
And it always just complicates things.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.240
I think of that game, like Mighty Bomb Jack.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.440
Was that the name of the game?

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:17.940
And that one was just like, yeah, it was just so bizarre because it was clearly Mario influenced, but it added in like Bomberman like tropes too.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.180
And it just didn't sell the idea enough.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.300
And I think that's the game. That's the problem with a lot of these games.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:29.240
You can't just be Super Mario Brothers one because then you're just trying to, you know, recreate a perfect game already.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.820
And that's going to be a problem for Super Mario Brothers two though, right?

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.520
I mean, like it can't be just Super Mario Brothers one again.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.300
Nintendo of America already said they won't take the official Japanese Super Mario Brothers two.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.540
So we get this game, which isn't quite the same thing as Super Mario Brothers one.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:52.380
Yeah. It's interesting that you mentioned that so many games are trying to, trying to, you know, are inspired by Super Mario Brothers one.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:59.380
And then all of a sudden Super Mario Brothers two feels like it's almost not inspired by Super Mario Brothers one other than some like little design elements and stuff.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:05.280
It feels like they kind of opened the, you know, they started over with their like basic rules of the game.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.560
Yeah. I think the most important thing to note is just even the way the game starts.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.400
You start after you choose your character, of course.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:17.540
You start and you just fall from a door in the sky and it drops you vertically.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:26.520
The idea that falling pits might not be pits anymore and that you are now like able to traverse both up and down as well as left and right.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:35.180
I think that's like an immediate thing that just, again, it's a Nintendo design philosophy thing, but it's the first way to let you know this is not Super Mario Brothers one.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.380
This ain't your daddy's Super Mario.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:43.040
So let's talk about the characters because we have, we have other than Mario this time.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.140
I mean, in the other game, you were able to be Luigi, but it was really just Mario.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.060
In this game, they actually give different character traits for Mario.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.640
He can jump the furthest.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:54.780
Luigi has like a high jump.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.040
Peach can float for a little bit.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:05.560
And Toad, as far as I understand, he can pull the vegetables out the fastest, but he's also just also the fastest character in terms of run speed.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.140
So what did you guys think of the characters?

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:08.580
Sam, you want to tell me about them?

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.880
Sure. Uh, I went for Toad at first because I thought it was cool.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.460
You could be Toad in a game, which I wouldn't really think of being except for Mario Kart.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:24.040
But, and I didn't, I couldn't really immediately tell what the differences were between Toad and Mario, for example.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.260
But then I tried Luigi out and he was just way, way too slippery for me.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.840
And I'm not great at platformers in the first place.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.440
So I'm mostly stuck with Peach because of the ability to fly and or glide.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:46.340
And I, I looked it up and there are some, like she, she picks up things the slowest, which I never really noticed because I mostly stuck with her because she can correct for my bad platforming.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.660
So that was my experience with it.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.360
I mean, I stuck mostly with Mario.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:57.720
Uh, I, I did like to, uh, I do like the feel of Peach for every level once in a while.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:03.260
Um, but, uh, I, I thought that Luigi was a little too floaty, so I, I, I stuck with the OG.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:10.520
It's interesting because, uh, I, I, you know, I've played this game very, very little when I was, when I was a kid because I didn't have it.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.580
But I remember like playing it at friends' houses and stuff.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:19.080
Um, and I always, just in my head, I was always like, well, Peach is the best character because, because of the ability to kind of hover.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.960
And like you said, Sam, it corrects for my platforming.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:25.920
But then when I tried Luigi, I actually found that I liked him better a little bit.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:33.940
I, I, I liked something about the, I can't really put my finger on it, but something about the floating with Peach kind of often like made me run into things more.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:41.440
And with Luigi, where it's just felt like a slower jump, I felt like I had a little more of that like reaction time control over it.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.560
Yeah, I was just gonna say, I played with Luigi for most of the game.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:50.060
And then by the end, I switched to Mario for a while and actually found a lot of advantages to Mario as well.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:55.880
Yeah, I think the thing about Luigi with that high jump is because of the vertical nature of the game too.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:05.480
A lot of times it helps to just be able to like jump over obstacles or, or go to another part that maybe Mario or Peach or Toad wouldn't be able to reach.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.060
But Luigi can with ease.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.840
Uh, but you know, there's a case for every single character here.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:20.640
And I think that's also something really cool because you can't design like a game around all four of those characters, but there's never really like a level that's like, oh, well, if you pick Toad, you're screwed on this level.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.120
Right. I would love to hear the, the, the case for Toad though.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.100
Uh, not, not saying that sarcastically.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:37.860
I, I just didn't play with him enough, but the one time I tried, I just felt like his, his biggest advantage was like he can jump just as high with or without holding something.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.360
But that's just not very high.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.020
I don't know. That's all I noticed.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:42.500
I do really like that though.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:49.660
That the, like I could just see that printed in like Times New Roman in like a, a game's journal.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.700
It's just the case for Toad.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:09:01.140
Abstract. I would say, I would say for Toad, because he can grab things and move around with them so easily.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.320
Maybe Toad is more of like the boss fighter character.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.140
You know, when you have to jump on Birdo's egg.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.340
I like that. And toss it back.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.440
Then Toad can, I guess in theory Toad does that the easiest.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:12.520
I guess that would make sense.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.680
Cause that's what I found after playing almost the whole game with Luigi.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.460
Once I switched to Mario, I was like, wow, boss fights are way easier with Mario.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.460
So I guess with Toad, it might even push that further in that direction.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:51.820
Right. And this is a new look for a lot of these characters too.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:57.800
Even Mario looks more like how present day Mario looks than anything we saw in Super Mario Brothers 1.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:03.920
So in a lot of ways, I know Sam, you were saying it's just interesting to be playing as Toad at all.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:09.740
But in a lot of ways, this is the game that really introduces this Mario like universe and canon.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:16.160
And a lot of things that we remember for these characters doing happened in this game more so than in Super Mario Brothers 1.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:18.860
For sure. Yeah.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:32.660
I mean, since I grew up with the Super NES, I always knew Mario as this much more like flourished character, I guess.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:36.140
Like it looks a bit more cartoony than it does pixelated.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:45.620
And they really softened a lot of the blockiness that the Super Mario Brothers 1 model had.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:53.600
And just across the board, the game looks a lot softer on the eyes, I would say.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:59.860
Yeah, I'd say they look close to Super Mario Brothers 3 character models.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.980
Which, I mean, I know that that's just like one of the most popular games out there.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.440
And that's the one that, you know, introduced me to the series.

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:13.380
And that's like, I think this game, even though it's very different than, you know, the gameplay style of Super Mario Brothers 1 and 3.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.900
It feels like it's, even though I know it was designed as a different game,

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:21.240
it does feel like it's moving some elements in the direction of Super Mario Brothers 3,

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:26.600
which is, you know, in the sense that it's, there's so many more secrets and, you know,

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:31.140
there's the design element that looks a little more like it and a little more of that, like,

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.480
adventure feel to it rather than just the platform feel to it.

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:40.000
Not that the first game only had a platform feel, but I just feel like it's moving closer to that adventure feel.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:45.040
I think a lot of the levels feel like puzzles instead of just random platforming.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:48.080
Yeah, I agree. We get more of that in Super Mario Brothers 3, too.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:54.620
Definitely. But I do think that in this game, it's, even if I, if I wouldn't say that it gets more necessarily puzzly,

00:11:54.940 --> 00:12:01.260
just the game, the design of the game makes it more, it's less about, like, reaction time

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:05.440
and more about, like, thinking about what your next move is than I think, I mean,

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.600
other than Super Mario Brothers 3, I'm not the biggest Mario fan.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.000
I mean, I was obsessed with Super Mario Brothers 3 itself.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:16.140
But from, so from my perspective, it does that more so than any of the other main Mario games.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:21.080
And, you know, I think that has a lot to do with the fact that in this version of, in Super Mario Brothers 2,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:23.120
you're able to go back as well.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:27.000
In Super Mario Brothers 1, once you hit a far enough point in the screen,

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:31.340
you couldn't turn back and explore whatever you had, you know, even the block that you had just passed.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.740
Like, now that's off limits.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:38.600
And so this game has more of an adventure vibe because you are able to just, at any particular moment,

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.860
slow down, take everything in.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.780
You're going to different worlds.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.880
You know, you start off in a grassy land and then next thing you know, you're in a desert.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.440
And nothing else really comes to mind right now.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:54.420
But I think, like, just the idea that each world is different is better than how Super Mario Brothers 1 handled

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:59.820
everything just being in, like, an overworld of the Mushroom Kingdom with the same looks over and over again.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:04.660
I think it also plays a part that there are times that you have to go back.

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:09.480
Like, if you're going to retrieve a key or something, you have to get back to where that key is used.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:16.240
And while there sometimes would be another exit, sometimes you'd have to backtrack through a screen.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:22.620
And that just wasn't part of the vocabulary of Super Mario Brothers 1.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:26.280
Yeah, that was a really interesting, like, element of the design, too,

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:29.320
is that, like, the levels aren't necessarily always linear.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:33.440
Like, there's, like, something, a lot of times there's a level where there's something you have to go figure out

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.760
on, like, how to progress.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:40.920
And even the fact that sometimes that includes using elements that have always,

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.280
up until this point, been for other purposes.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.180
I know there were a couple of them.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:48.480
I was actually, before we recorded, I was trying to scrub through videos of this

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.800
to remember some of the times that I noted it.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:56.280
And the only one I remember off the top of my head is there's one level where there's just a random Birdo.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:57.960
Not a boss fight.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:13:59.180
Just Birdo is just there.

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:06.640
And you realize that for the first time, you need to jump on its whatever, the eggs or whatever it is that it spits out.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.940
Not to pick up the egg and throw it back at him, but to jump on it and ride it across the level.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:16.540
And I thought that was really cool where it's like, oh, I just haven't, I've been programmed by the game to think about this one way

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:20.780
where if I stop and think about, like, what it's actually doing, I can utilize that for other purposes.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:22.640
And the game did that a few times.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.840
I wish I could think of the other examples that I, you know, noticed.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:28.740
There was one, there's vulture-y looking birds.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:36.000
There were a couple levels where, there's one level in particular where you basically just have to ride the bird for the entire time.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.040
That's right, yeah. And you ride it back, right?

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:39.060
Yeah. Well, yeah.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:43.880
And you have to jump to, like, jump onto things while you're riding it and then jump back onto it.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:46.040
And then jump to a different bird at one point.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:50.480
And there, I think there was a different level where you get to the end of the level and then there's nothing over there.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.940
But then a bird spawns going the other way and you have, you realize you have to jump back on it

00:14:54.940 --> 00:15:00.520
and go back to where you came from at the beginning of the level and you can jump over some barrier that was there.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:02.640
Yeah, I think that was in World 5, Sam.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:04.000
And I remembered that, too.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:09.200
And I thought that was just, like, a great way to communicate that because you're seeing them the whole time flying overhead.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:11.280
And, you know, you think of them as just an enemy.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:18.600
But it's not until you traverse the entirety of the map and get to the other side and there's just nothing there that you start to think, like,

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:20.680
okay, did I miss a door or something?

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:21.760
Like, did I do something wrong?

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:28.280
And it's just kind of because of how frequently they occur and you see them moving against you in the opposite direction,

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:32.060
it just kind of subtly communicates that in a way that you understand, like,

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.200
I probably should take this thing for a ride.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:38.400
Yeah. Did anyone get, like, a Mega Man vibe from Mario Bros.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:42.780
2? Like, that it had some inspiration in terms of just the way that the levels are laid out?

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:51.420
And I don't know if that's just because in Mega Man you were able to also go up and down and left and right as you choose and you weren't, like, locked behind anything.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.940
But I had this just feeling of this space.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:58.820
And maybe it had to do with also throwing projectiles that it felt like a Mega Man game in some ways.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.640
I can just see what you mean.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.480
I never felt that.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:09.480
But now that you say it, I think a lot of, there are some cosmetic things that could allude to Mega Man.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:14.700
I think the big one is just the way that the screen scrolls when you've hit the edge.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:19.340
That's the only part for me that was super Mega Man-y.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:20.500
Yeah, that's what I was going to say, too.

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:26.520
The way the screen scrolls, which I thought was another thing that made it, made the way you approach the game feel differently.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:28.400
Because it's not all about speed and precision.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:34.140
The way the screen scrolls, but also the fact that, like, you know, then you see all of the enemies present on the screen.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:41.800
There's no, like, surprise, you know, again, to that point of now you have time to just kind of breathe and think about, well, who do I really have to take out?

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:43.080
Can I just jump over everybody?

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:48.380
Do I have the right character to handle this thing that if I would do it a different way with Toad than I would with Mario?

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:53.360
I think a lot of that is just in the stage-by-stage design philosophy.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:17:00.980
That's kind of what I was thinking, where you really have to think about each encounter that you have and what the best way of approaching it is.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:11.740
Like, even when you're in those jars and you have to dig down what the best way of avoiding the Shy Guys is and, like, how you zigzag around them and stuff and planning your route back up.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:16.980
It kind of gives it a little bit more, like, a little bit more thought to the encounters than Super Mario 1 did.

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:20.480
Are you referring to when you're sort of digging through the sand?

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:28.780
Yeah. No, that – I never really – because I used to own this on, like, in the All-Star, like, cart.

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:36.640
Yeah. And I never really knew what to do with that, but it just clicked now that I'm, you know, an adult and have problem-solving abilities.

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:50.580
But I really enjoyed those because there was – I mean, it's not like it was super hard, but you were still engaged in, like, oh, what is the best – what is the optimal route here?

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:57.580
For sure. Dan, you know, we've been talking a lot about the characters that we play as, but, Sam, you're talking about the Shy Guys.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:02.860
I mean, those are now an iconic thing in the Mario universe, but they weren't in the first game.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:06.980
But then there's also enemies that were in the first game, like Goombas.

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:13.020
I mean, Goombas were the very first Mario – Super Mario anime, and they're nowhere to be found in this game.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:17.160
I think there's Koopa Troopa shells, but no actual Koopa Troopas.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:18.900
Yeah, it's just the shells. It's very strange.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:25.100
What did you guys think about this mix mash of, like, you know, new characters, familiar Mario tropes?

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:27.480
Like, did – I would imagine it would be welcomed.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:33.360
That it might feel like you're rehashing too much of the first game, you know, if you played this originally in 1988.

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:43.200
I would say – so, just kind of off track here, going back to me owning this in the All-Star collection.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:50.160
I played this as a very small child, like, you know, 7 or 8, 6, 7, 8.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:58.560
And I remember playing through Super Mario, Super Mario 2, Super Mario 3, and just feeling like 2 was the weirdest.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:03.060
And that 2 almost felt like some kind of fever dream.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:11.360
And I – just because, like, everything that is familiar to you, aside from the characters, is pretty much gone.

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:16.780
And the things that would work a certain way don't work that way anymore.

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:22.720
And I didn't know at the time that this was a, like, a reskin, pretty much.

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:30.860
But I guess, like, sort of in my mental space, I still sort of recognize the patterns of that.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:39.300
But – and so, I mean, looking back at it now, like, even now that I have the explanation in my head of why these things are different.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:47.600
So, it does still – it does still have this weird, like, it's not really Mario, but it is Mario feel to it.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.140
I don't know if that makes any sense.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:56.600
No, 100% it does because, you know, if you look at the three games in the collection, the way you played it as a kid and the way –

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:58.220
I remember playing it for the most part.

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:01.140
I did play the NES ones too, but I just remember from the All-Star collection.

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:10.820
When you play them like that, 3 is almost, like, learns nothing from 2 and goes back to the idea of just Mario being the main character that you play as.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:19.260
And, you know, there's emphasis on a vertical in a small way with the raccoon tail and stuff like that.

00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:22.160
But for the most part, you are just playing Super Mario Bros.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:26.300
1, you know, with additional power-ups and a sprawling world.

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.060
And obviously, it's a lot more complicated than that.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:29.560
But that's a time for another episode.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.460
Super Mario Bros. 2 has this whole, like, picking up vegetables and throwing them at enemies thing that we're just not going to see again.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:54.420
Yeah. Yeah, there's something – because, you know, I think that now, looking at it – like Sean said, looking at it now with the knowledge that this was Doki Doki Panic and it was reskinned to be a Mario game and it was actually made as a totally different game, just happened to be by – Miyamoto made this still, right?

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:55.480
So it's still by the same guy.

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.960
It's still in his – it's in his development team, yes.

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:04.920
Gotcha. But that, you know, it was – now looking at it, like, knowing, like, okay, this isn't going to be a traditional Mario game.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:08.220
I think it's a lot easier to appreciate how good this game is.

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:13.540
And I think if I were – if I bought this, you know, when it came out, expecting Super Mario Bros.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:16.460
2, I might have just had this biased disappointment towards it.

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:25.260
Kind of in the same way that if I, you know, if when – you know, if I was obsessed with Pokemon Red and Blue and then Pokemon Gold and Silver came out and it was just, like, a puzzle game or something.

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:28.760
And it could have been the best, coolest puzzle game in the world.

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:31.560
I'd still have been like, well, this isn't what I was looking for.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:36.900
So, like, I get why this one, you know, had a little bit of a reputation of being regarded as the weird one.

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:43.860
But I think then if you separate that and you look at it independently, you can really realize, like, oh, this is also, like, another really good game.

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:45.980
It just happens to be very different than its original.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:55.120
So it's like I have a mixed feeling about it because I do think when you do a sequel there should be a little more recognizable from the original game generally.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:24.640
I'll see you later.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:37.460
Well, why do you think they got rid of – and obviously, like, let's pretend that they can change these things from Doki Doki Panic.

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:41.460
Because if they really want to – I mean, this is their big flagship franchise.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:44.860
If they wanted to, I'm sure they could go into the game's code and change some things.

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:52.280
Why didn't they make it so that when your character, be it Mario or anybody else, when you jump on an enemy, it squashes them?

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:56.180
Like, that was the whole gameplay mechanic behind Super Mario Bros.

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:59.320
1. And I get that in Doki Doki Panic it doesn't happen.

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:04.560
But it's a little strange that you jump on top of these enemies and then you have to, like, just pick them up.

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:05.760
That's really tricky.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:17.320
I think that if – because if you change the skeleton – I mean, so you're working with the skeleton of a game and you decide to change, like, a pretty significant feature.

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:19.700
You've pretty much ruined the entire thing.

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:26.140
I don't think that this would work if they changed that line of code and got rid of the picking up of enemies.

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:27.740
I don't think this would work at all.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:31.100
So I think it's just they – like, they took a holistic approach.

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:37.220
They kept pretty much every part of the gameplay as it was.

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:41.840
And, like, they changed a couple of the items to look like Mario items.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:50.660
I think another question that could be posed, though, is why didn't they maybe take some of the sprites from some of the enemies that are new?

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:52.480
Maybe not the Shy Guys because those are cute.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:55.040
But – and change those into Goombas.

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:59.140
So I'm not really sure what the answer to that would be.

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:01.140
But I don't know.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:02.800
I guess it's worth discussing.

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:11.320
I would say that I think it makes total sense that nothing looks like it did in the previous game because nothing plays like it did in the previous game.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:18.360
And that you'll want to do – you'll want to approach situations with a certain mindset that you're used to from the first game.

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:22.900
And if you see things that kind of remind you of that, then you'll go back into that.

00:24:23.020 --> 00:24:23.900
That makes sense.

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:28.400
Super Mario 1 platformer mindset and kind of lose this.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.980
Like, all right, I have to pick up this guy so that I can throw him over here.

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:33.360
And then that will do this.

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:37.880
And that will do this. You know, it changes the way that you approach situations, I think.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:39.220
Yeah, I see that for sure.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:47.260
And I think that, like, now with my 2020 hindsight on this, I think it was cool that everything looks pretty much, for the most part, different.

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:48.920
It's a whole – you know, it's supposed to be a dream.

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:49.860
It's supposed to be a different world.

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:51.240
It introduced all these new characters.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:52.180
I think it's really cool.

00:24:52.400 --> 00:25:03.840
But I would – I mean, I can't see myself, if I were developing this, being brave enough to go away from what – from these enemies that were, like, so recognizable and successful and just that branding.

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:09.400
So, like, you know, kudos on them for sticking with that, even if that wasn't the reason.

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:11.040
I mean, maybe it was just easier to do it this way.

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:21.840
But kudos on them anyways because I think that they made the right call when it probably could have been seen as, like, the safer move to just make everything look like it did in the original game.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:30.980
Yeah, now that I think about it, if I were to step on a Goomba – if I jumped on a Goomba and it didn't crush, I would probably go insane.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:32.900
Yeah, you'd be very dissatisfied.

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:40.100
Yeah. You know, there are some Mario universe elements, though, in the game.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:42.420
There's the – we talked about the Koopa Troopa shells.

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:45.540
There's the Pal Blocks from Mario Brothers.

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:48.940
There's the Sparks from Donkey Kong Jr.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:54.720
and then there's the boss crab fight that kind of acts like Donkey Kong.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:26:00.900
So it's, like, none of those games are quite Super Mario Brothers 1, but they're still in this Mario universe and those things are added.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:07.580
So, you know, this stuff happens, but it doesn't quite feel like any other Mario game.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:12.940
Right. But I felt like, you know, as far as the Koopa Troopa shells and the Power Box, the Koopa Troopa shells, I don't know.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:18.460
I imagine in Doki Doki Panic they were just something else and they were like, oh, let's make those couple of times you pull this thing out.

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:21.240
Let's make it be something recognizable, like, easy enough.

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:28.920
And the Pal Blocks, I mean, maybe there weren't Pal Blocks in the original game, but that's also something that's, like, not necessarily gameplay breaking because you could put them wherever you want.

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:31.520
And it's – I didn't – they didn't think they were too frequent.

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.120
The last thing you said, the crab thing, was that similar to Donkey Kong?

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:44.220
Just in the sense of the layout of it, and I believe – I don't think he was throwing barrels, but whatever he was throwing acted like how the barrels acted.

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:48.060
I guess that's true, but that's different than adding, like, an element like the Pal Blocks or the Koopa Troopa.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:51.360
It's just more like – it just felt like an homage to me.

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:54.240
Gotcha. But we also have cool new characters.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:27:03.620
You know, the Shy Guys are awesome, but we also have the Bab-Oms and Pokies, those cactus characters, and Birdo, you know?

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:10.220
Yeah, Birdo still has some really weird, like, kind of nightmarish qualities to her.

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:14.800
I don't know if you guys were ever kind of, like, put off by Birdo before.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.700
I guess I can't really explain it, but –

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:21.260
Just a big hole in your face will do that, I guess.

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:26.320
Also, what is the thing that you pick up after you defeat Birdo?

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:27.540
Is it Birdo's stomach?

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:29.220
It's their soul.

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:30.280
That's just the orb.

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:32.100
Yeah, that's just part of Birdo?

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:34.480
Well, no, it's the eagle's orb, right?

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:35.840
Like, what is that eagle thing?

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:38.140
You've got to get swallowed by the eagle.

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:46.680
Yeah, it's a contextless, like – it's a contextless change that I don't even know if it makes sense in Doki Doki.

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:53.660
It probably does, but I – it just – it feels so much like a childhood nightmare.

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:56.580
It's funny to think of, like, Super Mario Bros.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:28:01.280
1 and then, like, afterwards the developers are like, oh, let's make the next one, like, a fever dream.

00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:03.440
Well, the first one was already pretty weird.

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:08.740
You, like, went – like, you made it, like, a fever dream within the world that's already a fever dream.

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:10.580
I like how in Super Mario Bros.

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:15.200
1 the little end-of-level minigame is how high can you jump on the flagpole?

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:18.120
And this one's like, feed the bird.

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:21.080
Feed the bird with yourself.

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:26.860
Yeah. There's just some strange moments in subcom, like, all over the place, right?

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:34.620
Because, you know, we talked about the key, but we didn't talk about the fact that there are these masks that will then, like, chase you throughout the remainder of the stage.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:36.200
Yeah. It was also terrifying.

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:38.820
I think that's the – yeah, the terrifying thing for me.

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:41.980
Until you realize that they're actually pretty easy to keep under control.

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:44.800
You just got to keep throwing the key away from you, and then they go away.

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:51.580
And I talked about the door that starts the whole game, you know, that you just, like, open up this door and you just fall.

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:53.660
But, yeah, that's just so strange, right?

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:54.780
Oh, yeah, that door, too.

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:55.760
That's a good one, Sean.

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:56.860
We should talk about that door.

00:28:56.960 --> 00:28:57.940
That's very strange.

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:03.500
Yeah. Like, I always felt like I was in a place I shouldn't be.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:05.520
Again, this is seven-year-old me.

00:29:05.520 --> 00:29:15.080
It has a lot of weird subconscious effects on me that probably don't affect everybody.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.400
But this game just feels haunted.

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.340
It feels more haunted than baseball.

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:25.920
And whenever you went to the Dark Realm, it kind of freaked me out as a kid.

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:29.460
Isn't the Dark Realm the only place where, like, regular Super Mario Bros.

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:31.920
music plays? Yeah, pretty much.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:34.840
Wait, that is the Dark Realm?

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:36.820
I guess I'm missing what we're talking about.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:43.580
Okay, remember when you pick up the chemicals and you just throw them on the ground and then a door opens up.

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:50.860
Then you go through that door and it's a shadow opposite version of the place where you just were, wherever you were.

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:55.580
And keep in mind that if you didn't pull any vegetables or anything, those things are still there.

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:59.460
And now they turn into red coins, also, I guess, from Mario Bros.?

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:01.840
That's right, yeah. I don't know why I was confused about what the Dark Realm is.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:04.060
There's only one thing that could possibly be Dark Realm.

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:07.260
You know, so I thought that was very strange.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:15.420
And I didn't quite understand that other than the mushroom that you can lift up to then get, like, an extra bar of health.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:17.820
Yeah. But how did that appear?

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:18.980
What are the rules for that?

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:25.580
I don't know. Maybe if you didn't disturb anything except for the plant that has the chemicals in it.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:28.960
I thought that they were actually planted in certain places in the level.

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:32.540
Like, if you threw the door in the right place, every time the mushroom would be in that spot.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:38.980
Yeah, because I have thrown the door at a place that the mushroom was also occupying that space.

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:40.180
I just couldn't pick up the mushroom.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:41.720
Huh. Okay.

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:45.000
You know, there's also the doors with the chemicals.

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:51.900
You can put those on certain vases, and that creates warp zones.

00:30:52.060 --> 00:30:53.860
Did you guys know there were warp zones in this game?

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:55.300
See, I didn't know that.

00:30:55.300 --> 00:31:00.000
I knew that there were, but I didn't know how to access them, or didn't really want to either, but...

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:02.380
I didn't even want to access the warper.

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:05.960
You didn't even want to go into the warper's scary places.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:09.520
So you put it on the vase, and then...

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:13.040
So you can't just put it on any vase.

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:16.660
You know, you can't just be like, oh, this is a vase, so I'm just going to put the door on top of that.

00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:19.440
It has to be on specific ones in specific levels.

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:20.480
On a specific vase.

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:23.080
If you put it, yeah, if you put it there, sure.

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:29.300
If you put it there, and then go down, you'll warp.

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:30.660
It'll just jump to the next thing.

00:31:30.740 --> 00:31:32.460
It'll be like, welcome to World 5.1.

00:31:32.780 --> 00:31:34.240
And it doesn't, you know, like, that's it.

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:36.160
You're just in there now. Spooky.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:41.500
I just think that, like, things like that are, you know, it's great that the warp zone continues,

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:46.140
but you just got to wonder, like, how would you know which vase to go through?

00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:52.220
Yeah, it's a little bit more ambiguous than Super Mario Bros.

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:56.720
It was like, even as a kid, I knew, like, oh, I kind of want to get above the level.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:32:01.660
So you're sort of drawn to that if it looks like you're able to.

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:07.300
Whereas in this, it's kind of like a random trial and error, like, very secret thing.

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:13.380
Yeah, and not even random because it's like the opposite of that because you put the door down,

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:16.080
and your first instinct is usually, I want to go through the door,

00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:20.520
but this is deciding not to go through the door and losing it instead.

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:24.780
Yeah. To try something else out, which I wouldn't think of normally.

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:29.820
No. And we joked about the eagle head, and I think that that is very strange

00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:33.440
and just another endless layer of the strangeness of this game.

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:39.780
Don't you think it's even stranger, though, in the final level when you have to, like, it becomes a boss?

00:32:41.980 --> 00:32:47.620
Yeah, that's very unsettling because at this point it's become something that you've finally grown to trust.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:49.560
Okay, I'll let you eat me, you know.

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:54.520
Every time I'll just climb into this bird's mouth, and then suddenly it's like, no, I am your enemy.

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:58.580
You know, I actually never, I haven't beaten the game.

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:00.380
I think I got to World 5.

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:01.840
Oh, gotcha, okay, yeah.

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:04.320
No, this is legit, like, the last stage of the game.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:06.820
Yeah, it's like right after you beat a bird, oh, right?

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:10.720
Yes. And then all of a sudden you're like, all right, I know what to do, I've done it every time,

00:33:10.720 --> 00:33:13.940
and then, like, the thing on the wall, the disembodied head of a bird,

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:17.040
just, like, blinks to life and starts flying around at you.

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:21.700
You'd hate it. I'm sure I would.

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:25.980
I'm sure I'd have actual nightmares about it if I actually beat this game as a kid.

